# verify/space.pict — this repository's input space, in Microsoft PICT's own syntax.
#
# cv-infra expands this into a covering array and runs verify/sim once per row,
# `repeats` times. It reads names and values; what any of them MEAN is ours.
#
# AXIS NAME -> ARGV. Every axis becomes one flag on verify/sim: `target: chair` is
# handed to the script as `--target=chair`, in the order declared here. So a name must
# match verify/sim's parser exactly and must be CLI-flag-safe (^[A-Za-z][A-Za-z0-9_-]*$,
# and never `help`/`h`). Values travel as TEXT — verify/sim writes them straight into
# verify/out/world.yaml and verify/oracle.py re-reads them off the same argv.
#
# THIS FILE IS THE COMMITTED ARRAY. The covering array is derived from this file at every
# run, and each case is identified by a hash of its own axis assignment, so a baseline
# follows a case across commits only as long as its values are untouched. Editing a value
# (or a name) retires the old case and starts the new one without a baseline — a rename
# is a deliberate act, not a formatting change.
#
# SIZE. Six axes = 2 * 3 * 3 * 3 * 3 * 2 = 324 combinations; the constraint at the bottom
# removes two thirds of the chair half. Pairwise (k=2) covers it in **12 rows**, measured
# 2026-09-22 with the pinned PICT build (`pict verify/space.pict /o:2` -> 12 data rows).
# One case measured 167 s wall on a cold cache (2026-09-23, etri6000; see the budget
# comment in .github/workflows/verify.yml) — the workflow's `budget: 10800` is sized
# against the 300 s mission ceiling plus a cold boot, not against that best case.
#
# PICT picks the CELL: it is discrete and combinatorial, so it answers "which combinations
# must be tried". "How much does one cell vary" is what `repeats` answers instead.

target:      person, chair
start_x:     -6.3, -6.0, -5.7
start_y:     -1.3, -1.0, -0.7
start_yaw:   1.4208, 1.5708, 1.7208
box_count:   0, 1, 2
desk_count:  0, 1

# A chair only reads as `chair` from the front (0.834 front / 0 behind), so its
# bearing is pinned; a standing person reads from every angle measured.
IF [target] = "chair" THEN [start_yaw] = 1.5708;
